    sectionType: "integrate",
    title: "SDA Theological Integration",
    content: "Adventists understand the church through a dual lens: it is both the universal body of all genuine believers across history and a visible, organized community with identifiable marks. This distinction matters because it prevents two opposite errors — reducing the church to an invisible, purely spiritual concept with no earthly accountability, or equating the church with a single institutional structure as though membership cards determine salvation.\n\n**Continuity with Israel.** The Adventist reading of 1 Peter 2:9-10 emphasizes that God has always had a covenant people. Abraham's call in Genesis 12:1-3 established a community through whom \"all the families of the earth\" would be blessed. The exodus community at Sinai became a \"kingdom of priests\" (Exodus 19:6). The New Testament church inherits this same calling — not as a replacement but as an expansion. The God who called Israel out of Egypt calls believers out of spiritual darkness into the same priestly vocation.\n\n**Christ as Head, not human authority.** Colossians 1:18 declares Christ as \"the head of the body, the church.\" Adventists affirm that no human leader, council, or institution can claim the authority that belongs to Christ alone. Church organization is necessary and biblical (as Acts 6 and the Jerusalem council in Acts 15 demonstrate), but it serves Christ's headship rather than substituting for it. This conviction guards against both authoritarian leadership and the opposite extreme of radical individualism that rejects any form of corporate accountability.\n\n**The church as witness in the great controversy.** Within the Adventist framework, the church is not merely a social institution — it is God's chosen instrument for demonstrating the viability of His government of love before the watching universe. Ephesians 3:10 says that through the church, \"the manifold wisdom of God\" is made known to \"principalities and powers in the heavenly places.\" The church's unity, holiness, and mission serve as living evidence that God's way produces flourishing, even in a fallen world.\n\n**Corrective boundary.** This belief guards against the idea that personal spirituality without community is sufficient. The biblical pattern is consistently corporate — believers are fitted together, called together, sent together. The lone-ranger Christian is a modern invention without scriptural support.",
