    content: "Adventist ecclesiology holds two truths in careful tension: the church is a living organism sustained by the Spirit, and it requires visible organization to carry out its mission faithfully. Neither truth can be sacrificed without distortion.\n\n**Organism: the church lives because Christ lives.** The body metaphor in Ephesians and Colossians is not decorative language. Adventists affirm that the church's vitality comes from its connection to Christ, not from its programs, budgets, or institutional reputation. A congregation that maintains every organizational structure but has lost its living relationship with Christ is, in Paul's framework, a body separated from its head — which is to say, a corpse. Spiritual renewal, prayer, Scripture study, and the indwelling Holy Spirit are the church's lifeblood.\n\n**Organization: the church structures itself for mission.** At the same time, Adventists reject the notion that organization is inherently unspiritual. The early church appointed deacons (Acts 6), established elders in every city (Titus 1:5), convened councils to resolve doctrinal disputes (Acts 15), and organized collections for the poor (1 Corinthians 16:1-2). Structure is the skeleton that gives the body shape and enables purposeful movement. The Adventist system of local churches, conferences, unions, and the General Conference is not a hierarchy of spiritual authority but a framework for coordinated mission and mutual accountability.\n\n**The bride being prepared.** Ephesians 5:27 speaks of a church \"not having spot or wrinkle.\" Adventists understand this as an eschatological promise — the church's perfection is a future reality that Christ Himself will accomplish. In the meantime, the church lives in the tension between its calling and its condition. Honest acknowledgment of the church's failures is not disloyalty; it is part of the sanctification process. The Great Controversy framework adds depth here: the church's imperfections are exploited by the enemy and observed by the universe, making the process of corporate sanctification a matter of cosmic significance.\n\n**Corrective boundary.** This doctrine guards against two errors: ecclesial triumphalism (claiming the church is already perfect or that membership guarantees salvation) and ecclesial nihilism (dismissing the organized church as irrelevant or corrupt beyond redemption). Christ loves the church as it is and is transforming it into what it will be.",
