    id: "w3s-13-3-3",
    lessonId: "w3l-13-3",
    sectionType: "integrate",
    title: "SDA Theological Integration",
    content: "Adventist mission is inseparable from Adventist identity. The denomination was born out of prophetic conviction, and its global expansion reflects the three angels' call to reach \"every nation, tribe, tongue, and people.\" Several distinctive features characterize the Adventist approach to mission:\n\n**Holistic mission.** Adventists have historically understood the gospel commission to include not only preaching but also healing, educating, and serving. The denomination operates one of the largest Protestant educational systems in the world, along with hospitals, clinics, and disaster relief organizations. This reflects the pattern of Jesus's own ministry, which integrated teaching, healing, and proclamation. The gospel addresses the whole person — body, mind, and spirit.\n\n**Urgency without panic.** 2 Peter 3:11-13 calls for holy living in light of the approaching day of God. Adventists affirm that Christ's return is imminent — not in the sense of predictable timing but in the sense of readiness. This urgency produces diligence, not anxiety. The remnant does not set dates or manufacture crises; it lives faithfully, serves generously, and proclaims clearly, trusting God's timing while taking responsibility for its own faithfulness.\n\n**Every member a missionary.** Acts 1:8 was not addressed to professional evangelists alone but to the entire community of believers. Adventist ecclesiology affirms the priesthood of all believers — every baptized member is a witness, whether or not they hold a formal office. The local church is the primary unit of mission, and every congregation is a mission outpost in its neighborhood.\n\n**Hastening the return.** The Adventist understanding of Matthew 24:14 — \"this gospel of the kingdom will be preached in all the world as a witness to all the nations, and then the end will come\" — creates a unique missionary dynamic. The church's faithfulness in proclamation participates in the fulfillment of eschatological conditions. This is not manipulation of divine timing but cooperation with God's revealed plan.\n\n**Corrective boundary.** This teaching guards against two distortions: mission reduced to social activism without the gospel message, and mission reduced to preaching without compassionate service. Biblical mission is always both — word and deed, proclamation and presence.",
